{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"a30457f2-f89c-4a9a-ae5b-125d51203c9e","title":"Radians: arc length on a circle"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a30457f2-f89c-4a9a-ae5b-125d51203c9e","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Radians measure an angle using distances on a circle. Start with a circle of radius r, measured from its center.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Radians: angle from arc length"},{"op":"figure","id":"circle_fig","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-2.6,2.8],"y":[-2.6,2.8],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"circle","id":"circ","center":[0,0],"r":2},{"kind":"point","id":"center","at":[0,0],"label":"O"},{"kind":"point","id":"start","at":[2,0]},{"kind":"segment","id":"radius","from":{"at":"center"},"to":{"at":"start"},"label":"r"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a30457f2-f89c-4a9a-ae5b-125d51203c9e","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Turn the radius through an angle. The curved distance swept along the circle is the arc length s, not the straight-line distance between endpoints.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"arc_step","on":"circle_fig","marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"finish","at":[0,2]},{"kind":"segment","id":"radius_end","from":{"at":"center"},"to":{"at":"finish"}},{"kind":"arc","id":"swept_arc","center":{"at":"center"},"r":2,"start":0,"end":90,"arrow":true,"label":"s"}]},{"op":"text","id":"arc_note","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.1},"content":"s follows the circle"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a30457f2-f89c-4a9a-ae5b-125d51203c9e","step":{"id":"s3","script":"The angle in radians is arc length divided by radius. One radian means the arc length equals one radius.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"angle_step","on":"circle_fig","marks":[{"kind":"arc","center":{"at":"center"},"r":0.55,"start":0,"end":90,"label":"θ"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"definition","anchor":{"el":"arc_note","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"\\theta=\\frac{s}{r}\\quad\\text{(radians)}"},{"op":"equation","id":"one_rad","anchor":{"el":"definition","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"s=r\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\\theta=1\\text{ rad}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"a30457f2-f89c-4a9a-ae5b-125d51203c9e","step":{"id":"s4","script":"For this quarter-circle, a two-meter radius gives an arc of pi meters. Dividing gives pi over two radians: the same angle as ninety degrees.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"values","on":"circle_fig","marks":[{"kind":"text","at":[1,-0.4],"text":"2 m"},{"kind":"text","at":[1.95,1.85],"text":"π m"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"example","anchor":{"el":"one_rad","side":"below","gap":0.12},"latex":"\\theta=\\frac{\\pi\\,\\mathrm{m}}{2\\,\\mathrm{m}}=\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\text{ rad}=90^\\circ"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"If the arc length equals three times the radius, what is the angle in radians?","expected_gist":"3 radians, because s/r = 3"}}}
